db/ref 1079 #uuid "fbb3031c-a5cc-577f-8ba0-340b2c59edc7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526000 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186525913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526000 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159927" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526000 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526000 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Exp}(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ The log-likelihood function of the sample $4,\\,6,\\,9,\\,7,\\,2,\\,1$ is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +b\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526000 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2e0fcfa7-37ec-568d-b873-d7a1d1d3286a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526000 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=6$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =4+6+9+7+2+1=29\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=6.$ Therefore,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\cdot 29 \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(-29)\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=6+(-29)=-23.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526006 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186525979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526006 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159929" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526006 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526006 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Exp}(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ The log-likelihood function of the sample $3.1,\\,6.5,\\,4.8,\\,7.2$ is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +b\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526006 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "33ce93bc-9b71-578c-a5f0-f916b3a30de2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526006 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =3.1+6.5+4.8+7.2=21.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=4.$ Therefore,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\cdot 21.6 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(-21.6)\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=4+(-21.6)=-17.6.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526007 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186525939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526007 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159905" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526007 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526007 #edb/ref 1071 "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{10}$ be I.I.D. random variables with probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter. For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{10},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}≈11.72.\n$$\n\nGiven that the log-likelihood function of the sample is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -b(\\theta +1)\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b,$ rounded to two decimal places?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526007 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "374585a1-b6ca-5c27-b592-db80b72d2e1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526007 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=10$ independent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x;\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-(\\theta +1)}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i} \\\\  & =n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}≈11.72$ and $n=10.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ≈10\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -(\\theta +1)\\cdot 11.72 \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -11.72(\\theta +1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b≈10+11.72=21.72.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526023 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186525854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526023 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159949" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526023 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417]