e independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=3$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{x-\\theta }{\\sigma })}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{(x-\\theta )^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =2.5+0.2+(-1.1) \\\\  & =1.6 \\\\ C & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(2.5)^{2}+(0.2)^{2}+(-1.1)^{2} \\\\  & =7.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=3.$ Therefore,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}(7.5-2\\theta \\cdot (1.6)+3{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =-1.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+(-3.75)+(1.6)\\theta +(-1.5){\\theta }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=-3.75+(1.6)+(-1.5)=-3.65.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526071 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186525919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526071 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159916" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526071 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526071 #edb/ref 1071 "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ be I.I.D. random variables with probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter. The log-likelihood function of the sample $1.3,\\,2.9,\\,3.3,\\,4.5$ is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -b(\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b,$ rounded to two decimal places?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526071 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b1c528bb-28eb-5c70-b173-6d36e7f34b5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526071 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1},\\,x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x;\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta {x}^{-\\theta -1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{l